.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/estree-walker Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "estree-walker"
.PP
Simple utility for walking an \fRESTree\fP-compliant AST, such as one generated by \fRacorn\fP\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.PP
.nf
npm i estree\-walker
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
var walk = require('estree\-walker')\&.walk;
var acorn = require('acorn');

ast = acorn\&.parse(sourceCode, options); // https://github\&.com/acornjs/acorn

walk(ast, {
  enter(node, parent, prop, index) {
    // some code happens
  },
  leave(node, parent, prop, index) {
    // some code happens
  }
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
Inside the \fRenter\fP function, calling \fRthis\&.skip()\fP will prevent the node's children being walked, or the \fRleave\fP function (which is optional) being called\&.

.PP
Call \fRthis\&.replace(new_node)\fP in either \fRenter\fP or \fRleave\fP to replace the current node with a new one\&.

.PP
Call \fRthis\&.remove()\fP in either \fRenter\fP or \fRleave\fP to remove the current node\&.
.SH "Why not use estraverse?"
.PP
The ESTree spec is evolving to accommodate ES6/7\&. I've had a couple of experiences where \fRestraverse\fP was unable to handle an AST generated by recent versions of acorn, because it hard-codes visitor keys\&.

.PP
estree-walker, by contrast, simply enumerates a node's properties to find child nodes (and child lists of nodes), and is therefore resistant to spec changes\&. It's also much smaller\&. (The performance, if you're wondering, is basically identical\&.)

.PP
None of which should be taken as criticism of estraverse, which has more features and has been battle-tested in many more situations, and for which I'm very grateful\&.
